"""Original quota-recovery exercise with a dated official source."""
BODY = '''<section class="page-hero task-hero"><p class="eyebrow">FREE AI TOOLS / KEEP YOUR PROGRESS</p><h1>What should you save before a free AI limit runs out?</h1><p class="lede">Save the original input, checked facts, unfinished draft and one next action in your own file. Keep uncertain claims separate. If generation is already blocked, save these notes manually from the conversation you can still read.</p></section><article class="prose">
<h2>Identify the blocked step first</h2><p>A usage limit, a conversation-length limit and an unavailable export option are different problems. Record the exact message and any reset time displayed in your account. A new chat is not a guaranteed way to restore an account allowance. Continue only within the provider's permitted access; save the work now and resume when access is available.</p><p>Claude's <a href="https://support.claude.com/en/articles/9797557-usage-limit-best-practices" rel="noopener">official usage guidance</a> lists message length, attachment size, conversation length and tool use among the factors affecting usage. It recommends planning requests and giving concise, specific context. This does not establish a fixed number of free messages for your account. Use the reset time shown in your own account.</p>
<figure class="editorial-figure"><img src="/assets/editorial/ai-quota-handoff.svg" width="760" height="1080" alt="Save original input, checked facts, unfinished draft and one next action before resuming."/><figcaption>Keep checked facts separate from unfinished wording.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>Save four parts of the task</h2><div data-original="packet"><p>Use four small files or four clearly labelled sections in one document. Name the version and date. Keep the original material and its source labels under Input. Under Checked facts, write what you compared with that material. Save unfinished wording under Draft and mark anything still uncertain. Under Next step, name one task and how you will know it is done.</p><p>Save the notes in your own file and reopen it. A chat title or a link alone is insufficient when the next tool cannot access the old conversation. Preserve relevant source excerpts with their labels, rather than expecting an assistant to reconstruct the missing context. Remove private identifiers before transferring material to another service.</p><blockquote>VERSION / DATE:<br>GOAL AND FINISH:<br>ORIGINAL INPUT WITH SOURCE LABELS:<br>CHECKED FACTS AND THEIR SOURCE LABELS:<br>DRAFT / UNCHECKED CLAIMS:<br>UNKNOWN OR CONFLICTING DETAILS:<br>NEXT ACTION — ONE TASK:<br>HOW TO CHECK THE RESULT:</blockquote><p>If you still have access to generation, you may ask for a summary using these headings. Compare it yourself before saving. If generation is blocked, copy the relevant parts manually. Do not spend your last available request polishing prose when the original input has not yet been preserved.</p></div>
<h2>Practice with our fictional Cedar workshop</h2><div data-original="fixture"><p>The following workshop notes and faulty draft are invented for practice. Watch the two counts: confirmed participants and people waiting for a seat.</p><blockquote>S1 — Room note: capacity 8 seats.<br>S2 — Booking note: 6 confirmed participants; 3 people on the waiting list.<br>S3 — Coordinator note: Jo prepares the handout. Session date UNKNOWN.<br>D1 — Unchecked draft: “Nine participants are confirmed for Friday. Lee will send the handout.”</blockquote><p>Save S1, S2 and S3 as input. Keep D1 in the draft section with an unchecked label. Do not turn D1 into a verified summary merely because it reads fluently. Someone who never saw the chat should be able to find each count and assignment in these saved notes.</p><p>Our checked section is: capacity 8 (S1); confirmed 6 and waiting 3 (S2); handout owner Jo and date UNKNOWN (S3). Remaining seats are 8 minus 6, which equals 2, derived from S1 and S2. Nine people have expressed interest across confirmed and waiting categories; that is not nine confirmed bookings. Do not allocate the remaining seats without a separate instruction.</p></div>
<figure class="editorial-figure"><img src="/assets/editorial/ai-quota-check.svg" width="760" height="1080" loading="lazy" alt="Cedar exercise: 8 seats minus 6 confirmed leaves 2; 3 waiting are separate; Jo owns the handout; the date stays unknown."/><figcaption>Keep the waiting list separate from confirmed bookings.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>Resume with one small request</h2><div data-original="resume"><p>For this exercise, the next action is a short coordinator update, not a booking decision or a message to be sent. Use this request after attaching the checked notes:</p><blockquote>Using only S1–S3 and the checked arithmetic, draft a coordinator update of at most 60 words. Separate confirmed and waiting counts. Preserve UNKNOWN for the date and Jo as the handout owner. Do not allocate seats or send anything. Flag conflicts in D1 instead of adopting them.</blockquote><p>The answer should say: “Capacity is 8 seats. Six participants are confirmed and three are waiting, leaving two seats before any new allocation. Jo prepares the handout. Session date: UNKNOWN. The earlier draft's nine confirmed participants, Friday date and Lee assignment are unsupported by S1–S3.”</p><p>Check four things before continuing: 6 remains the confirmed count; 3 remains the waiting count; Jo keeps the handout assignment; the date remains UNKNOWN. Check the derived 2 separately against 8 minus 6. A corrected count with an invented Friday date is still an unreliable summary. Repair the specific claim and keep the previous version as a record.</p><p>Once the notes pass these checks, request only the next useful unit of work. If the task can be completed in your editor using already checked facts, finish it manually. If it requires further generation, wait for permitted access or evaluate a suitable alternative with its current terms and data settings. Whichever tool you use, keep checking against the same input.</p></div>
<h2>Questions when work stops</h2><details><summary>What if I cannot send another prompt?</summary><p>Save readable input and drafts manually. Mark what you checked and what remains unknown. You can save your work without generating another summary.</p></details><details><summary>Will a new conversation restore my allowance?</summary><p>Do not assume so. Read the account's exact limit notice. A shorter conversation may help a context problem, while an account usage limit can still apply.</p></details><details><summary>Should I copy the entire old conversation?</summary><p>Keep an archive if needed, but supply the relevant input, evidence, draft and next action for the task you are resuming. Omit unrelated material and private details.</p></details><details><summary>Did you test this example in an AI tool?</summary><p>No. These are invented notes with a worked answer so you can check your own result.</p></details>
<h2>Check the saved destination too</h2><p>After recovery, <a href="/ai-tools/check-free-ai-export/">verify that you can save, reopen and edit the final result</a>. For document input, <a href="/ai-tools/scanned-pdf-to-text/">check OCR text before asking questions</a>. For your first task, use the <a href="/start/">beginner workflow</a>.</p>
</article><section class="prose"><h2>Source and review scope</h2><p>Official usage guidance reviewed 2026-10-06. The saved-note template, Cedar exercise and worked answer were written for this guide. They do not measure a product’s usage allowance. <a href="/free-ai/">Return to free AI tools</a>.</p></section>'''
def publish_quota_guide(page, cfg):
    page('/ai-tools/save-free-ai-progress/', 'Save your progress before a free AI limit | '+cfg['brand'], 'Save your notes, separate facts from draft wording and resume with the next step.', BODY, lastmod='2026-10-07')
